window.docContents[195].resolve({"/Hoare2/#Hoare2":{"contents":"open scoped Com MyGetElem Assertion HasTriple HasEval\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn Hoare, we defined Hoare Logic's proof rules and\nproved them sound. In this chapter, we put those rules to work:\nverifying Imp programs by formalizing the informal proof sketches we\ncall decorated programs.Such proofs require specifications, i.e., the pre- and post-conditions\nthat explain what programs are supposed to do. Let's play with\nwriting specifications.\n\n\n\nWrite down a Hoare-triple specification for the following program:X := 2;\nY := X + X\n{{True}} c {{X = 2 ∧ Y = 4}}\n\n\nWrite down a (useful) specification for the following program:X := X + 1;\nY := X + 1\n∀ m, {{ X=m }} c {{ X = m+1 ∧ Y = m+2 }}\nThis is more interesting than {{ True }} c {{ Y > X }}.\n\nWrite down a (useful) specification for the following program:if X ≤ Y then\n  skip\nelse\n  Z := X;\n  X := Y;\n  Y := Z\nend\nOne obvious spec is {{True}} c {{X≤Y}}, but this loses the fact that\nthe final values\nare either the same as the initial or else swapped, so then a spec\nwith parameters seems preferable.  There's also a question\nabout whether to say something about Z in the postcondition.\n\nWrite down a (useful) specification for the following program:X := m;\nY := X + X\n{{True}} c {{X = m ∧ Y = 2 * m}}\n\n\nWrite down a (useful) specification for the following program:X := m;\nZ := 0;\nwhile X ≠ 0 do\n  X := X - 2;\n  Z := Z + 1\nend\n{{True}} c {{X = 0 ∧ Z = (m + 1) / 2}}\nHere / is natural-number division: the loop subtracts 2 from X\neach time around, so it runs ⌈m / 2⌉ times whether m is even or\nodd, thanks to truncated subtraction turning the odd case's last 1\ninto 0 rather than going negative.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Hoare Logic","header":"6. Hoare2: Hoare Logic, Part II","id":"/Hoare2/#Hoare2"}});